// src/std/parse/parse.zen
//
// The parser's whole surface, in one file of starred re-exports.
// A folder root is its folder's public surface and nothing else: everything
// reachable through `parse` is listed here, and what's absent — bounded
// scans, poison builders, the seven-way `value_shape` fork — is
// module-private on purpose; no caller has any use for them.
//
// WHAT A CONSUMER ACTUALLY NEEDS is four names. Build a `Parser` over a
// token stream, call `module`, read the tree and the diagnostics back
// off it:
//
//     lexed = scan(alloc, Source(file: "src/main.zen", text: bytes)).try();
//     p ::= alloc.Parser(lexed);
//     m = p.module("main").try();
//     p.diags.len.match({ .. });      // rejected, or not
//     p.tree                          // every node m refers to
//
// The tree is read back off the Parser, not returned beside the module,
// because an `Ast` owns `Vec`s: a copy diverges from the original the
// first time either grows, so there is exactly one and the Parser holds it.

// what a parse produces, and what it produces instead when it fails
Diag*, Note*, diag*, diag_at* = std.parse.parse_diag

// what a parse consumes: `lex`'s alphabet, re-exported so a sibling names
// it once. A CONSUMER should import `lex` directly for `scan`/`Source`/
// `Lexed` — those are the lexer's surface. parse_token.zen records where
// the interface this parser was written against disagreed with the real one.
Token*, TokenKind* = std.parse.parse_token

// the machine, and the one budget it enforces
Parser*, Mark*, MAX_DEPTH* = std.parse.parser

// the entry point, and the pieces a consumer that is not parsing a
// whole file might want: `zen fmt` reads a module, an LSP completion
// re-parses one expression, and a test builds one type.
module*, declaration* = std.parse.parse_decl
block* = std.parse.parse_stmt
expr* = std.parse.parse_expr
type* = std.parse.parse_type
pattern* = std.parse.parse_pattern
arms* = std.parse.parse_match
